<com:TContent ID="body">
	<h1>Building an AJAX Chat Application</h1>
	<com:RequiresVersion Version="3.1" />
	<p class="block-content">This tutorial uses the PRADO web application framework's <a href="?page=Database.ActiveRecord">Active Record</a> and <a href="?page=ActiveControls.Home">Active Controls</a> to build a chat web application. It assumes that you are familiar with PHP and have access to a web server that serves PHP scripts. The application uses the following PRADO features.
	</p>
	<ul id="u1" class="block-content">
		<li>Building a custom User Manager class.</li>
		<li>Authenticating and adding a new user to the database.</li>
		<li>Using ActiveRecord to interact with the database.</li>
		<li>Using Active Controls and callbacks to implement the user interface.</li>
		<li>Separating application logic and application flow.</li>
	</ul>

	<p class="block-content">The AJAX chat lets several users talk through their web browsers. The application has two pages: a login page that asks the user for a nickname, and the main chat page. You can try the application <a href="../chat/index.php">locally</a> or at the <a href="https://www.pradoframework.net/demos/chat/">PRADO website</a>. The main chat page is shown below. <img src="<%~chat1.png%>" class="figure" alt="The chat page: a message area, a list of current users, a text area for a new message, and a Send button." />
	</p>

	<h1>Download, Install and Create a New Application</h1>
	<p class="block-content">The download and installation steps are similar to those in
	the <a href="?page=Tutorial.CurrencyConverter#download">Currency converter tutorial</a>.
	</p>

    <p class="block-content">
    The best way to install PRADO is <a href="https://getcomposer.org/">through composer</a>
    <br/>If you don't use composer yet, first install it:
    </p>
    <com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content">
    curl -s https://getcomposer.org/installer | php
    php composer.phar install
    </com:TTextHighlighter>
    <p class="block-content">
    Then, create the application structure using composer:
    </p>
    <com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content">
    composer create-project pradosoft/prado-app chat
    </com:TTextHighlighter>
    <p>
    The command creates a new directory named
    <tt>chat</tt> in your current working directory.
    You may need to change to the appropriate directory
    first.
    </p>

	<p class="block-content">The command creates the directory structure and the minimal files, including <tt>index.php</tt> and <tt>Home.page</tt>, of a PRADO web application. Point your browser at the <tt>index.php</tt> script in the <tt>chat</tt> directory. The page shows the message "Welcome to Prado!"
	</p>

	<h1>Authentication and Authorization</h1>
	<p class="block-content">Each user chooses a unique username. The main chat page denies access to anonymous users, so a user logs in first. Create the <tt>Login</tt> page with the following code, saving <tt>Login.php</tt> and <tt>Login.page</tt> in the <tt>chat/protected/pages/</tt> directory, beside the <tt>Home.page</tt> that <tt>composer create-project</tt> created.
	</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;?php
class Login extends TPage
{
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content">
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <title>Prado Chat Demo Login</title>
</head>
<body>
&lt;com:TForm&gt;
    <h1 class="login">Prado Chat Demo Login</h1>
    <fieldset class="login">
        <legend>Please enter your name:</legend>
        <div class="username">
            &lt;com:TLabel ForControl="username" Text="Username:" /&gt;
            &lt;com:TTextBox ID="username" MaxLength="20" /&gt;
            &lt;com:TRequiredFieldValidator
                ControlToValidate="username"
                Display="Dynamic"
                ErrorMessage="Please provide a username." /&gt;
        </div>
        <div class="login-button">
            &lt;com:TButton Text="Login" /&gt;
        </div>
&lt;/com:TForm&gt;
</body>
</html>
</com:TTextHighlighter>
	<p class="block-content">The login page contains
	a <com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.TForm" Text="TForm" />,
	a <com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.WebControls.TTextBox" Text="TTextBox" />,
	a <com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.WebControls.TRequiredFieldValidator" Text="TRequiredFieldValidator" />
	and a <com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.WebControls.TButton" Text="TButton" />. The page looks like the following after a style sheet is applied. <img src="<%~chat2.png%>" class="figure" alt="The login page: a username field with a Login button." /> A click on <tt>Login</tt> with an empty username textbox shows an error message, because the <com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.WebControls.TRequiredFieldValidator" Text="TRequiredFieldValidator" /> requires text in the textbox.
	</p>
<h2>Securing the <tt>Home</tt> page</h2>
<p class="block-content">A user who opens <tt>Home.page</tt> before logging in is sent to <tt>Login.page</tt> first. Start with a <tt>chat/protected/application.xml</tt> configuration file that names the application and sets its mode. The framework classes autoload, so the file imports nothing; later steps add to it.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="xml" CssClass="source block-content">
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<application id="Chat" Mode="Debug">
</application>
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">The same configuration in the PHP format:</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
<?php
return [  'application' => ['id' => 'Chat', 'Mode' => 'Debug'],
];
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
Next, we add a <tt>chat/protected/pages/config.xml</tt> configuration file to
secure the <tt>pages</tt> directory.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="xml" CssClass="source block-content">
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<configuration>
  <modules>
    <module id="users" class="TUserManager" />
    <module id="auth" class="TAuthManager" UserManager="users" LoginPage="Login" />
  </modules>
  <authorization>
    <allow pages="Login" users="?" />
    <allow roles="normal" />
    <deny users="*" />
  </authorization>
</configuration>
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">The same configuration in the PHP format:</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
<?php
return [
  'modules' => [
    'users' => ['class' => 'TUserManager'],
    'auth' => [
      'class' => 'TAuthManager',
      'properties' => ['UserManager' => 'users', 'LoginPage' => 'Login'],
    ],
  ],
  'authorization' => [
    ['action' => 'allow', 'pages' => 'Login', 'users' => '?'],
    ['action' => 'allow', 'roles' => 'normal'],
    ['action' => 'deny', 'users' => '*'],
  ],
];
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
The authentication uses the default classes described in <a href="?page=Advanced.Auth">Authentication and Authorization</a>. The first authorization rule allows anonymous users, denoted by the <tt>?</tt> question mark, to access the <tt>Login</tt> page. The second rule allows users with the role "normal", defined later, to access every page. The last rule denies everyone else. The rules apply on a first-match basis.
</p>

<p class="block-content">A request for the <tt>Home</tt> page through <tt>index.php</tt> now redirects to the <tt>Login</tt> page.
</p>

<h1>Active Record for <tt>chat_users</tt> table</h1>
<p class="block-content">The <com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Security.TUserManager" Text="TUserManager" />
class provides a read-only list of users. The chat adds and logs in new users at run time, so it uses its own user manager class. First, set up a database with a <tt>chat_users</tt> table and an Active Record class for it. The demo uses <tt>sqlite</tt>, which keeps the demo easy to distribute; MySQL or PostgreSQL work the same way. The <tt>chat_users</tt> table is defined as follows.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="text" CssClass="source block-content">
CREATE TABLE chat_users
(
	username VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY,
	last_activity INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT "0"
);
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
Next, define the <tt>ChatUserRecord</tt> class and save it as <tt>chat/protected/App_Code/ChatUserRecord.php</tt>, creating the <tt>App_Code</tt> directory as well. Save the sqlite database file as <tt>App_Code/chat.db</tt>.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
class ChatUserRecord extends TActiveRecord
{
    const TABLE='chat_users';

    public $username;
    public $last_activity;

    public static function finder($className=__CLASS__)
    {
        return parent::finder($className);
    }
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
Active Record needs a default database connection before the <tt>ChatUserRecord</tt> class can run. In <tt>chat/protected/application.xml</tt>, import the classes in the <tt>App_Code</tt> directory and add an <a href="?page=Database.ActiveRecord">Active Record configuration module</a>. The <tt>App_Code</tt> path is the one import the application needs; the framework classes autoload.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="xml" CssClass="source block-content">
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<application id="Chat" Mode="Debug">
  <paths>    <using namespace="Application.App_Code.*" />
  </paths>
  <modules>
    <module class="TActiveRecordConfig" EnableCache="true"
        Database.ConnectionString="sqlite:protected/App_Code/chat.db" />
  </modules>
</application>
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">The same configuration in the PHP format:</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
<?php
return [
  'application' => ['id' => 'Chat', 'Mode' => 'Debug'],
  'paths' => [
    'using' => [      'Application.App_Code.*',
    ],
  ],
  'modules' => [
    'activerecord' => [
      'class' => 'TActiveRecordConfig',
      'properties' => [
        'EnableCache' => 'true',
        'Database.ConnectionString' => 'sqlite:protected/App_Code/chat.db',
      ],
    ],
  ],
];
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<h2>Custom User Manager class</h2>
<p class="block-content">A custom user manager module extends the <tt>TModule</tt> class and implements the <tt>IUserManager</tt> interface. The <tt>getGuestName()</tt>, <tt>getUser()</tt> and <tt>validateUser()</tt>
methods are required by the <tt>IUserManager</tt> interface.
We save the custom user manager class as <tt>App_Code/ChatUserManager.php</tt>.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
class ChatUserManager extends TModule implements IUserManager
{
    public function getGuestName()
    {
        return 'Guest';
    }

    public function getUser($username=null)
    {
        $user=new TUser($this);
        $user->setIsGuest(true);
        if($username !== null && $this->usernameExists($username))
        {
            $user->setIsGuest(false);
            $user->setName($username);
            $user->setRoles(array('normal'));
        }
        return $user;
    }

    public function addNewUser($username)
    {
        $user = new ChatUserRecord();
        $user->username = $username;
        $user->save();
    }

    public function usernameExists($username)
    {
        $finder = ChatUserRecord::finder();
        $record = $finder->findByUsername($username);
        return $record instanceof ChatUserRecord;
    }

    public function validateUser($username,$password)
    {
        return $this->usernameExists($username);
    }
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
The <tt>getGuestName()</tt> method returns the name for a guest user; the application does not use it.
The <tt>getUser()</tt> method returns a <tt>TUser</tt> object if the username
exists in the database, the <tt>TUser</tt> object is set with role of "normal"
that corresponds to the <tt>&lt;authorization&gt;</tt> rules defined in our
<tt>config.xml</tt> file. </p>

<p class="block-content">The <tt>addNewUser()</tt> and <tt>usernameExists()</tt>
method uses the ActiveRecord corresponding to the <tt>chat_users</tt> table to
add a new user and to check if a username already exists, respectively.
</p>

<p class="block-content">Next, change the <tt>config.xml</tt> configuration to use the custom user manager class, by changing the <tt>&lt;module&gt;</tt> with <tt>id="users"</tt>.</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="xml" CssClass="source block-content">
<module id="users" class="ChatUserManager" />
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">The same configuration in the PHP format:</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
'users' => ['class' => 'ChatUserManager'],
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<h1>Authentication</h1>
<p class="block-content">Authentication asks the user for a unique username. A <com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.WebControls.TCustomValidator" Text="TCustomValidator" /> validates that the username is unique, and an <tt>OnClick</tt> event handler on the login button creates the user.</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:TCustomValidator
    ControlToValidate="username"
    Display="Dynamic"
    OnServerValidate="checkUsername"
    ErrorMessage="The username is already taken." /&gt;

...

&lt;com:TButton Text="Login" OnClick="createNewUser" /&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
In <tt>Login.php</tt>, add the following two methods.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
function checkUsername($sender, $param)
{
    $manager = $this->Application->Modules['users'];
    if($manager->usernameExists($this->username->Text))
        $param->IsValid = false;
}

function createNewUser($sender, $param)
{
    if($this->Page->IsValid)
    {
        $manager = $this->Application->Modules['users'];
        $manager->addNewUser($this->username->Text);

        //do manual login
        $user = $manager->getUser($this->username->Text);
        $auth = $this->Application->Modules['auth'];
        $auth->updateSessionUser($user);
        $this->Application->User = $user;

        $url = $this->Service->constructUrl($this->Service->DefaultPage);
        $this->Response->redirect($url);
    }
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
The <tt>checkUsername()</tt> method asks the <tt>ChatUserManager</tt> module, registered in <tt>config.xml</tt> under the ID "users", whether the username is taken.
</p>
<p class="block-content">
The <tt>createNewUser</tt> method adds the user when validation passes, that is, when the username is not taken. It then logs the user in manually:</p>
<ul id="u2" class="block-content">
	<li>It obtains a <tt>TUser</tt> instance from the custom user manager with <tt>$manager->getUser(...)</tt>.</li> <li>It stores the user object in the current session through the <tt>TAuthManager</tt>.</li> <li>It sets the <tt>Application</tt>'s user instance to the new user object.</li>
</ul>
<p id="finally" class="block-content">
Finally, it redirects the client to the default <tt>Home</tt> page.
</p>

<h2>Default Values for ActiveRecord</h2>
<p class="block-content">A login attempt now produces an error message like
"<i>Property '<tt>ChatUserRecord::$last_activity</tt>' must not be null as defined
by column '<tt>last_activity</tt>' in table '<tt>chat_users</tt>'.</i>". The <tt>$last_activity</tt> property was null when the record was inserted. Either the column gets a default value in the table, or the <tt>ChatUserRecord</tt> class supplies one. This tutorial takes the second option and adds a getter and a setter for the <tt>last_activity</tt> property to <tt>ChatUserRecord</tt>.

</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
private $_last_activity;

public function getLast_Activity()
{
    if($this->_last_activity === null)
        $this->_last_activity = time();
    return $this->_last_activity;
}

public function setLast_Activity($value)
{
    $this->_last_activity = $value;
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
The public <tt>$last_activity</tt> property becomes the private <tt>$_last_activity</tt>, with an underscore after the dollar sign.
</p>

<h1>Main Chat Application</h1>
<p class="block-content">The main chat page uses a simple layout: a panel for the chat messages, a panel for the user list, a textarea for a new message, and a button that sends it. A <tt>TJavascriptLogger</tt> at the end of the form shows callback activity during development.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content">
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <title>Prado Chat Demo</title>
<style>
.messages
{
    width: 500px;
    height: 300px;
    float: left;
    border: 1px solid ButtonFace;
    overflow: auto;
}
.user-list
{
    margin-left: 2px;
    float: left;
    width: 180px;
    height: 300px;
    border: 1px solid ButtonFace;
    overflow: auto;
    font-size: 0.85em;
}
.message-input
{
    float: left;
}

.message-input textarea
{
    margin-top: 3px;
    padding: 0.4em 0.2em;
    width: 493px;
    font-family: Verdana, Geneva, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;
    font-size: 0.85em;
    height: 40px;
}
