</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
The template has two active controls, a <com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.ActiveControls.TActiveTextBox" Text="TActiveTextBox" /> and a <com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.ActiveControls.TActiveButton" Text="TActiveButton" />, and a <com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.WebControls.TJavascriptLogger" Text="TJavascriptLogger" /> that shows how the active controls work.
</p>

<h2>Exploring the Active Controls</h2>
<p class="block-content">Before the chat buffer is set up, this step shows how a callback updates the current page. First, add an <tt>OnClick</tt> event handler for the <tt>Send</tt> button.

</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:TActiveButton ID="sendButton" CssClass="send-button"
	Text="Send" OnClick="processMessage"/&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
Then add the handler method in a new <tt>Home.php</tt> file.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
class Home extends TPage
{
    function processMessage($sender, $param)
    {
        echo $this->userinput->Text;
    }
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
Text typed in the textbox and sent with the button now appears in the <tt>TJavascriptLogger</tt> console.
</p>

<p class="block-content">The <com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.ActiveControls.TCallbackClientScript" Text="TCallbackClientScript" /> class, available through the <tt>CallbackClient</tt> property of the current <tt>TPage</tt>, appends content to the message panel. For example:
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
function processMessage($sender, $param)
{
    $this->CallbackClient->appendContent("messages", $this->userinput->Text);
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
This updates part of the existing page during a callback, and it is the primary technique of the chat application.
</p>

<h1>Active Record for <tt>chat_buffer</tt> table</h1>
<p class="block-content">A message to all connected users is buffered once per user, in the database. The <tt>chat_buffer</tt> table is defined as follows.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="text" CssClass="source block-content">
CREATE TABLE chat_buffer
(
	id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
	for_user VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL,
	from_user VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL,
	message TEXT NOT NULL,
	created_on INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT "0"
);
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
The corresponding <tt>ChatBufferRecord</tt> class is saved as
<tt>App_Code/ChatBufferRecord.php</tt>.

</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
class ChatBufferRecord extends TActiveRecord
{
	const TABLE='chat_buffer';

    public $id;
    public $for_user;
    public $from_user;
    public $message;
    private $_created_on;

    public function getCreated_On()
    {
        if($this->_created_on === null)
            $this->_created_on = time();
        return $this->_created_on;
    }

    public function setCreated_On($value)
    {
        $this->_created_on = $value;
    }

    public static function finder($className=__CLASS__)
    {
        return parent::finder($className);
    }
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<h1>Chat Application Logic</h1>
<p class="block-content">The chat logic comes next. First, a received message is saved into the chat buffer for <b>all</b> current users. This logic lives in the <tt>ChatBufferRecord</tt> class.

</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
public function saveMessage()
{
    foreach(ChatUserRecord::finder()->findAll() as $user)
    {
        $message = new self;
        $message->for_user = $user->username;
        $message->from_user = $this->from_user;
        $message->message = $this->message;
        $message->save();
        if($user->username == $this->from_user)
        {
            $user->last_activity = time(); //update the last activity;
            $user->save();
        }
    }
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
The method finds all current users through the <tt>ChatUserRecord</tt> finder, saves a copy of the message for each one, and updates the sender's last-activity timestamp.
</p>

<p class="block-content">The next piece retrieves a user's messages from the buffer. It loads every message for the username and formats each one, escaping the output to prevent cross-site scripting. It then deletes the loaded messages and any older messages left in the buffer.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
public function getUserMessages($user)
{
    $content = '';
    foreach($this->findAll('for_user = ?', $user) as $message)
        $content .= $this->formatMessage($message);
    $this->deleteAll('for_user = ? OR created_on < ?',
                          $user, time() - 300); //5 min inactivity
    return $content;
}

protected function formatMessage($message)
{
    $user = htmlspecialchars($message->from_user);
    $content = htmlspecialchars($message->message);
    return "<div class=\"message\"><strong>{$user}:</strong>"
                  ." <span>{$content}</span></div>";
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
The formatted list of current users comes from this method in the <tt>ChatUserRecord</tt> class. It first deletes users who have been inactive for a while.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
public function getUserList()
{
    $this->deleteAll('last_activity < ?', time()-300); //5 min inactivity
    $content = '<ul>';
    foreach($this->findAll() as $user)
        $content .= '<li>'.htmlspecialchars($user->username).'</li>';
    $content .= '</ul>';
    return $content;
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<div class="note"><b class="tip">Note:</b>
For simplicity the Active Record classes format the messages. A larger application formats them with PRADO components, such as a <tt>TRepeater</tt> in the template or a custom component.
</div>

<h1>Putting It Together</h1>
<p class="block-content">The next step wires the application flow together. In <tt>Home.php</tt>, the <tt>Send</tt> button's <tt>OnClick</tt> handler now uses the application logic.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
function processMessage($sender, $param)
{
    if(strlen($this->userinput->Text) > 0)
    {
        $record = new ChatBufferRecord();
        $record->message = $this->userinput->Text;
        $record->from_user = $this->Application->User->Name;
        $record->saveMessage();

        $this->userinput->Text = '';
        $messages = $record->getUserMessages($this->Application->User->Name);
        $this->CallbackClient->appendContent("messages", $messages);
        $this->CallbackClient->focus($this->userinput);
    }
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
The handler saves the message to the chat buffer, loads the messages for the current user, and updates the client-side message list through the callback response.
</p>

<p class="block-content">At this point the application works, with a minimal interface. Two browsers can exchange messages whenever <tt>Send</tt> is clicked.
</p>

<p class="block-content">The next part improves the user experience. First, the page shows a list of current users. The following method in <tt>Home.php</tt> can run whenever a callback event is raised, for example when the user clicks <tt>Send</tt>.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
protected function refreshUserList()
{
    $lastUpdate = $this->getViewState('userList','');
    $users = ChatUserRecord::finder()->getUserList();
    if($lastUpdate != $users)
    {
        $this->CallbackClient->update('users', $users);
        $this->setViewstate('userList', $users);
    }
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<p class="block-content">The messages and the user list also need periodic updates as users join and messages arrive from other users, so the message list refreshes as well.</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
function processMessage($sender, $param)
{
    ...
    $this->refreshUserList();
    $this->refreshMessageList();
    ...
}

protected function refreshMessageList()
{
    //refresh the message list
    $finder = ChatBufferRecord::finder();
    $content = $finder->getUserMessages($this->Application->User->Name);
    if(strlen($content) > 0)
    {
        $anchor = (string)time();
        $content .= "<a href=\"#\" id=\"{$anchor}\"> </a>";
        $this->CallbackClient->appendContent("messages", $content);
        $this->CallbackClient->focus($anchor);
    }
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
The anchor, with <tt>time()</tt> as its ID, is a focus point: when the message list grows long, the focus call scrolls the list to the latest message.
</p>

<p class="block-content">A user who has been inactive for about five minutes is sent back to the login page. The check can run at any stage of the page life cycle; this tutorial adds it to <tt>onLoad()</tt>.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
public function onLoad($param)
{
    $username = $this->Application->User->Name;
    if(!$this->Application->Modules['users']->usernameExists($username))
    {
        $auth = $this->Application->Modules['auth'];
        $auth->logout();

        //redirect to login page.        $this->Response->redirect($this->Service->constructUrl($auth->LoginPage));
    }
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<h1>Improving User Experience</h1>
<p class="block-content">The remaining details are to poll for new messages and refresh the user list periodically. The page polls the server with a
<com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.ActiveControls.TTimeTriggeredCallback" Text="TTimeTriggeredCallback" />
control in <tt>Home.page</tt>, which calls the <tt>refresh</tt> handler in <tt>Home.php</tt> every 2 seconds.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:TTimeTriggeredCallback OnCallback="refresh"
	Interval="2" StartTimerOnLoad="true" /&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
function refresh($sender, $param)
{
    $this->refreshUserList();
    $this->refreshMessageList();
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<p class="block-content">The final piece is a script in <tt>Home.page</tt>. When the user presses <tt>Enter</tt> in the textarea, the script sends the message without a click on <tt>Send</tt>.

</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="javascript" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:TClientScript&gt;
Event.observe($("&lt;%= $this->userinput->ClientID %&gt;"), "keypress", function(ev)
{
    if(Event.keyCode(ev) == Event.KEY_RETURN)
    {
        if(Event.element(ev).value.length > 0)
            new Prado.Callback("&lt;%= $this->sendButton->UniqueID %&gt;");
        Event.stop(ev);
    }
});
&lt;/com:TClientScript&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
The <a href="?page=Advanced.Scripts">Introduction to Javascript</a> section describes the script API.
</p>

<p class="block-content">This completes the tutorial.
</p>

</com:TContent>
